# Lessons from implementing Literate literately

Literate programming begins with a compelling reversal: arrange a program for
understanding, then derive the arrangement required by tools. Implementing a
literate-programming environment in its own language tests that idea at a scale
where both its strengths and its weaknesses become difficult to hide.

The strongest lesson is not that every program should become a book. It is that
explanation can be treated as an engineering structure. When concepts, invariants,
code, diagrams, tests, and generated artifacts are allowed to challenge one
another, the result can be easier to reason about than any one representation.
That result is never automatic. It requires continuing editorial work.

## Explanatory order is an architectural tool

A conventional source tree encourages explanations to follow modules. A literate
book can instead begin with the problem a boundary solves, introduce the shared
model, and only later show how several modules realize it. That freedom is most
valuable when a concern crosses files: deterministic snapshots, output safety,
source mapping, publication identity, or process isolation.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Question[Human question] --> Model[Conceptual model]
    Model --> Invariant[Invariant and tradeoff]
    Invariant --> Fragments[Selected implementation fragments]
    Fragments --> Files[Generated source files]
    Invariant --> Tests[Tests and acceptance evidence]
```

The arrows show a desired direction of explanation, not the order in which a
feature must be coded. The question gives the model a purpose; the invariant joins
prose, implementation, and tests. Generated files remain essential products, but
they no longer dictate the reader's first encounter with the idea.

This can improve architecture itself. A responsibility that cannot be named without
reference to accidental storage is often not yet well understood. A parent fragment
that does not read as a coherent outline may reveal misplaced responsibilities.
Writing for a reader becomes a form of design review.

## Fragment names form a design vocabulary

A good fragment name does more than identify text. It gives authors and readers a
phrase for discussing an intention: capture a project snapshot, reject a stale
reply, reserve every destination, or map one exact occurrence. Those phrases can
appear in prose, diagrams, tests, and parent fragments.

This vocabulary makes cross-cutting behavior visible. It also creates pressure for
honest boundaries. A fragment called `handle result` says little; a fragment called
`reject a reply from an obsolete snapshot` states both action and reason.

The pressure can be misused. Giving every few lines a polished name does not make
them conceptual. Over-fragmentation increases navigation and lets the author avoid
deciding which statements form one responsibility. The useful test is not fragment
length but explanatory independence: can the name carry real meaning in the
chapter and in the assembled outline?

## The shared model earns its central place

A self-describing tool is tempted to grow a separate interpretation for every
feature: one parser for generation, another for preview, a regular expression for
navigation, and a conversion-specific scanner for print. That path produces quick
local progress and long-term disagreement.

Literate's more durable architecture is a captured project interpreted into shared
fragment and publication models. Generation, inspection, screen rendering, print
rendering, and language projection then add policies appropriate to their roles.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Snapshot[Captured project] --> Shared[Shared parsed meaning]
    Shared --> Generate[Generate complete outputs]
    Shared --> Inspect[Inspect fragments]
    Shared --> Screen[Present on screen]
    Shared --> Print[Prepare print publication]
    Shared --> Project[Project generated language]
```

The fan-out is the important relationship. It does not mean every consumer accepts
the same incomplete input or uses the same presentation. It means they disagree by
explicit policy rather than accidental parsing.

This lesson extends beyond literate programming. Whenever several tools discuss the
same source, a renderer-neutral semantic center is usually more valuable than a
collection of similar adapters.

## Determinism makes the system discussable

Stable input, order, identities, mappings, diagnostics, and bytes do more than make
tests pass. They let different tools and people refer to the same event. A source
location in a diagnostic, a contribution in a fragment index, and a segment in a
language projection can all be traced back to one captured project.

Without determinism, failures become stories about timing: perhaps a directory was
enumerated differently, a late reply won a race, or a generated file reflected a
mixture of two edits. With deterministic semantics, disagreement is evidence of a
different input, a policy boundary, or a defect that can be isolated.

Self-hosting sharpens this benefit. A generation fixed point makes drift between the
book and its generator observable. It does not prove correctness, but it gives the
project a strong answer to a foundational question: does the fresh program reproduce
the source state that claims to define it?

## Failures deserve as much design as success

Many of Literate's most important decisions concern what must not happen:

- no partial files after a graph error;
- no mapped edit across an ambiguous occurrence;
- no publication callback before whole-document validation;
- no unbounded allocation from a protocol length;
- no claim of rollback when recovery failed;
- no claim that a temporary directory is a sandbox.

These negative guarantees are easier to understand when presented as part of the
main architecture rather than scattered among exception handlers. Literate
programming is well suited to this because a chapter can place the failure model
before the implementation paths it constrains.

The lesson is broader than “validate input.” Each boundary must identify the
authority being withheld. A malformed preview enhancement may lose only that
enhancement. An uncertain generated edit must lose permission to modify source. An
incomplete generation plan must lose permission to touch every output. Fail closed
at the scope where uncertainty could become harm.

## Completeness and explanation pull in different directions

A self-hosted book must retain every line needed to assemble the program. A readable
book must not give every line equal narrative weight. Imports, registrations,
protocol fields, and host adapters are important for completeness, but placing all
of them in the main argument can make implementation order displace explanatory
order.

This tension is not solved by deleting detail. It is managed through layers:

1. the main chapters explain motivation, models, invariants, algorithms, and major
   boundaries;
2. themed appendices retain assembly maps and exact integration contracts;
3. generated source presents the compiler's complete view;
4. tests and contributor references preserve executable examples and operational
   detail.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Main[Main narrative: understanding] --> Concept[Concepts and invariants]
    Appendix[Themed appendices: lookup] --> Mechanics[Assembly and protocols]
    Generated[Generated source: tooling] --> Complete[Complete executable order]
    Tests[Tests: evidence] --> Contracts[Concrete accepted behavior]
    Concept --> Program[One maintained system]
    Mechanics --> Program
    Complete --> Program
    Contracts --> Program
```

No layer is disposable, and no layer is sufficient alone. Auditability requires a
path to exact mechanics. Readability requires that the reader is not forced down
that path before understanding why it matters.

## The book can drift while every test passes

Executable tests detect many kinds of disagreement, but prose can become obsolete
without changing a byte of behavior. A diagram can preserve an old ownership
boundary. A chapter can describe a temporary workaround as though it were a design
principle. A fragment name can remain technically connected while no longer
matching its responsibility.

This is the central maintenance lesson: a literate program needs editorial tests as
well as executable ones. Reviewers should ask whether the prose works when code is
skipped, whether diagrams answer stated questions, whether terms are introduced
before use, and whether limitations remain proportional to the evidence.

The reverse drift is possible too. A beautiful explanation can conceal incomplete
assembly or an untested safety claim. Generated diffs, fixed-point verification,
and focused boundary tests keep the narrative answerable to the program.

## Implementation order will try to become book order

Features usually enter a project through practical pressure: a module is added, a
host needs an adapter, or a protocol gains another message. It is natural to place
new explanation beside the code that happened to change. Over time, that accretion
can turn a book into a chronological source tour.

A mature literate program must periodically restore conceptual order. That does not
require narrating every rearrangement or preserving development history in the main
text. It means asking afresh what a reader needs to know first and moving mechanical
completeness to a layer where it remains available without controlling the story.

This work cannot be delegated entirely to generation. Generation can find missing
fragment references and stale outputs, while publication validates supported links
to included documents and headings. Arbitrary Markdown links are not universally
checked. No automated pass can determine that a paragraph introduces a concept too
late or that two chapters make the same argument under different names.

## Accessibility can strengthen precision

Opening a technical idea in ordinary language is not a retreat from rigor. It forces
the author to say what the mechanism is for before presenting names and formulas.
Once the reader has that foothold, precise terms, diagrams, coordinate systems, and
bounds become easier to understand.

For example, “two tools count positions differently and may highlight the wrong
word” gives source-coordinate encoding a human consequence. The exact UTF-16 rule
can then be stated without mystery. “A partial generated program may combine new
and old modules” gives all-or-nothing planning a reason before transaction terms
arrive.

The durable pattern is intuition, model, invariant, implementation, and consequence.
Different chapters may emphasize different steps, but omitting the first two turns
technical precision into unnecessary exclusion.

## Self-hosting should create humility

A tool that can generate itself is impressive, but the fixed point is not a proof
that the design is wise or the program correct. Two implementations can reproduce
the same mistaken output. A book can generate perfectly while explaining poorly.
A passing bootstrap can coexist with a broken editor command.

Self-hosting is most useful when treated as one strong check in a larger argument.
It verifies continuity of authority: the fresh generator agrees with the book and
checked-in outputs. Tests, host acceptance, security review, publication inspection,
and reader review establish different claims.

This humility improves technical writing. Each claim should name its evidence and
its boundary. Doing so makes limitations useful design information instead of
fine print.

## Continue both the editorial and technical practice

Maintaining Literate means improving both sides of the method. Technical work may
seek richer but still exact source mappings, clearer explicit identities where
edit-stability matters, broader tested host support, and resource accounting across
more expensive boundaries. Editorial work strengthens cross-chapter transitions,
examples, accessible algorithm explanations, and appendices that remain easy to
audit as the program grows.

Those efforts should be judged by questions larger than feature count:

- Is the book still the clearest route to understanding the program?
- Can a contributor identify the authority for a behavior without guessing?
- Do failures preserve coherent state and explain what was withheld?
- Can generated consequences be reviewed without reading every implementation line?
- Are unsupported cases omitted honestly rather than approximated dangerously?
- Do screen, print, editor, and command-line views still discuss one project model?

The answer will never remain permanently “yes” without maintenance. Architecture
changes, external tools evolve, and explanations accumulate. Literate programming
turns that continuing responsibility into visible work rather than eliminating it.

## A continuing practice

The deepest lesson is that a literate program is not source code with more comments,
and it is not a book that happens to contain compilable examples. It is a maintained
agreement among explanation, executable fragments, generated artifacts, and
evidence.

When that agreement is healthy, the book can reveal why the program has its shape,
the program can hold the book accountable to exact behavior, and generation can
connect the two without making either secondary. When it is neglected, the same
machinery can produce a complete but unreadable source archive.

Literate therefore ends not with a claim that the method is finished, but with a
standard for its future: keep the explanation authoritative, keep the boundaries
honest, keep the generated consequences inspectable, and keep revising the route by
which another person can understand the whole.
